---
import LegalPage from "../../components/LegalPage.astro";
import { SUPPORT_AI_LIVE, anthropicAddition, privacyParagraph } from "../../content/support-words";
import QabotPrivacy from "../../components/QabotPrivacy.astro";
import RecapsPrivacy from "../../components/RecapsPrivacy.astro";
import { RECAP_AUDIO_LIVE, RECAP_SHARE_LIVE, RECAPS_LIVE } from "../../content/recap-words";
import { QABOT_LIVE } from "../../content/qabot";
import { initiativeLine, storyStatesLine } from "../../content/privacy-channels";
import {
  deletionWarnings,
  geminiPrivacy,
  keepAfterStopPaying,
  minimumAge,
  operator,
  paymentProvider,
  retentionRows,
} from "../../content/legal";
---

<LegalPage
  title="Privacy"
  description="What DMbot keeps about you, who sees it, how long we keep it, and how to delete it."
  heading="Privacy"
  summary={[
    "DMbot only records people who said yes.",
    "We don't keep the sound of your voice: it is turned into text and the sound is thrown away (unless you give a separate yes in a test server).",
    "DMbot uses what is said and typed to get better. You can turn that off in My Account. It does not stop recording.",
    "Everyone in your Discord server can read what was written down.",
    "We keep your Discord account number, your name, your email and a list of your servers while you are signed in, so you can sign in and use your plan.",
    "We don't sell anything about you, and there are no ads.",
    "You can stop being recorded at any time, and you can delete your account from My Account.",
  ]}
>
  <h2>Who looks after your information</h2>
  <p>
    DMbot is run by {operator.name}. Write to {operator.email} about anything on this page.
  </p>

  <h2>What we keep</h2>
  <h3>When you sign in to this website</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Your <strong>Discord account number</strong>, your <strong>Discord name</strong> and the
      <strong>email</strong> Discord gives us (only if Discord has confirmed it is yours). We use the
      email for the emails listed under "Email from DMbot" below, and to fill in the payment page
      when you pick a plan. We keep your Discord name so a personal link we send you can greet you
      by it.
    </li>
    <li>
      The <strong>email our payment company has for your payments</strong>, once you have paid. We
      keep it next to the Discord one so a move code can go to the address you pay with.
    </li>
    <li>
      The <strong>names and numbers of the Discord servers you are in</strong>, and whether you
      manage them, so we can show you the ones that use DMbot. We keep this only while you are
      signed in.
    </li>
    <li>
      You can also sign in with a link we email you. It works once and ends after 15 minutes. We
      keep only a scrambled copy of it, not the link itself, until then. When you ask for a link,
      our answer is the same whether or not the email has an account.
    </li>
    <li>
      A personal link from us holds your <strong>DMbot ID</strong> (your account number) and a
      secret part that stops working after 30 days. With it, the Prices and Say hello pages greet
      you by name and show your experience points (the minutes DMbot has run for you). Without
      the link, those pages show nothing about you. Don't share it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Sign-in uses Discord. We never see your Discord password. Discord gives us a one-time
      sign-in pass. We use it once to read your account, then cancel it. We never keep it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>If you were given free access</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Your <strong>Discord account number</strong>, each time free access was given, changed or
      taken back, and when, so we can see who had free access. We keep this record after you
      delete your account.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>When DMbot is in your game</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Your yes.</strong> When you press "I consent", we keep the date, the wording you
      agreed to, and how you agreed. That's how we prove you said yes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>What was said.</strong> The words of people who said yes, written down as text, with
      the time they were said. Nobody else is ever recorded, and bots are never recorded.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your campaign.</strong> The names, places and notes your table makes, the game
      master's notes and settings, and any rulebook text the game master shares. That
      includes which items are the only one of their kind and who carries what, and whether the game
      master or the bot said so. A backup holds it, and it is deleted when the campaign is deleted.
      An adventure file is the one exception. DMbot keeps its text, not the file, for that campaign
      only. It stays until the game master removes it or the campaign is deleted. A backup holds
      only its name.
      <strong>What DMbot makes from a session belongs to the campaign.</strong> That means a recap,
      story facts, notes for the game master, and anything else DMbot writes from what was said.
      It is part of the campaign, and is deleted only with the campaign.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>What was typed.</strong> While DMbot is listening, it also reads what people who
      said yes type in the table's chat: the voice channel's chat, one more channel the game
      master picks, and the dmb-chat channel where anyone can ask a quick rules question. Typed
      lines go in the written record, marked as typed. Questions put to DMbot, and its answers
      about the game, go in the written record too, marked as such.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Names at the table.</strong> DMbot notices what people are called at the table,
      like "Mike", only from people who said yes to being recorded. The game master checks those names. If you
      press 🗂️ Menu, then Stop recording me, DMbot forgets the names it picked up about you. Names
      your game master typed in themselves stay.
    </li>
    {
      initiativeLine.live && (
        <li>
          <strong>{initiativeLine.lead}</strong> {initiativeLine.text}
        </li>
      )
    }
    {
      storyStatesLine.live && (
        <li>
          <strong>{storyStatesLine.lead}</strong> {storyStatesLine.text}
        </li>
      )
    }
    <li>
      <strong>Deleting transcripts.</strong> Anyone who said yes can delete every line they said
      in a server, from every past game: press 🗂️ Menu in DMbot's private message to you, then
      🗑 Delete my transcripts. This can't be undone. It does not remove other people's lines, the story notes
      your game master kept, or copies someone already downloaded. A game master can also choose how long a campaign keeps its transcripts:
      forever, 1 year, 90 days or 30 days. Older ones are deleted within a day. A session whose
      transcript was deleted stays in the list, marked as deleted. Recaps (short summaries of a
      game) stay.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your voice is not kept.</strong> The sound is sent to be written down, then thrown
      away. It is never saved, unless you give a separate yes to "Save my voice for tests", which
      only some test servers ask.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>When you write notes on a name</h3>
  <p>
    Each person or group the table knows can have its own page on this website. It shows only what
    the table already sees, never the DM's secrets; the DM's
    own note on a name is shown to the DM only. The page also has a box called My notes. What you
    write there is for you alone: no other player and no DM can see it on DMbot. DMbot never uses
    it, and it is never put in a transcript, a backup or the campaign's story memory. You can
    download all your notes in a campaign, or delete them, from the NPC page. If a name is
    no longer listed, your note on it is kept for 30 days, then deleted. Your notes are deleted when
    the campaign is.
  </p>

  <h3>When you use the Say hello page</h3>
  <p>
    Your message is posted on GitHub, where anyone can read it. If you tell us how to reach
    you, we keep that with your message for a year; only the team sees it. To have a post
    taken down, ask on the same page.
  </p>

  <h3>When you write to the team</h3>
  {
    SUPPORT_AI_LIVE ? (
      <p>{privacyParagraph()}</p>
    ) : (
      <p>
        From your account page, you can write to the DMbot team. That message goes only to the
        team, privately, and is not posted anywhere public. The team replies by email to the
        address on your account, if it has one. We keep it for 12 months, then delete it.
        After you send it, your account page shows you its subject, when you sent it and whether it
        was answered. You can open it there to read the whole conversation: your message and the
        replies. On our website, only you and the team can read it. If you delete your account, we delete your
        messages too.
      </p>
    )
  }

  <h3>When you pay</h3>
  <p>
    Payments go through {paymentProvider}. They are the seller, and they hold your card or
    PayPal details; we never see them. When you pick a plan, we send them your email so their
    payment page already has it. They tell us your plan, whether it's paid, and when it
    renews.
  </p>

  <h2>Who can see it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>What was said</strong> can be read and downloaded by everyone in the Discord server
      where the game is played (downloads come with paid plans). The question you say yes to
      tells you this.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The game masters' (DMs') notes</strong>: each campaign picks who sees them. Only the
      game masters; players who choose to peek (the usual setting, with a spoiler warning first);
      or everyone in the server. When a player peeks, the game masters can see who peeked.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Backups</strong> of a paid campaign hold the campaign, including the game master's
      secrets, but not what was said, and anyone in the server may download one. This keeps a
      campaign safe if its game master leaves.
    </li>
    <li>Only people in your server can open your campaign in DMbot. Campaigns never share information, except house rules a game master chooses to share (below).</li>
    <li>
      If a game master chooses to share their house rules, only the rules themselves (and the link to their rules document, if they added one) can be read by someone who has the share code. The story, names, notes and what was said are never shared. Tables that use the rules keep their own copy. A table that uses them can suggest a rule back to the game master who shares them. A suggestion carries only the rule's words (and the rule it would replace), never who sent it, and the game master's side keeps no record of the sender.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="email-from-dmbot">Email from DMbot</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Which email.</strong> A move code goes only to the email you paid with. A sign-in
      link goes to the address you type, if it is the one you paid with or the one Discord has for
      you. The Try It emails go to the email Discord gave us.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>What we send.</strong> A sign-in link and a move code, only when you ask for one, and
      two Try It emails: one on the last day of Try It, and one if you get 30 more days.{
        RECAPS_LIVE ? " A game master also gets an email when a recap is ready or could not be written (see Recaps below)." : ""
      } Nothing
      else.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Stopping emails.</strong> Each Try It email has a link to stop emails like it. We keep
      a list of the Discord account numbers that used it, with the date, so we do not send them
      another. This list stays even after you delete your account, so we never email you again
      after you said stop. Sign-in links and move codes have no such link, because you asked for
      them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A short note of what was sent.</strong> For each email we keep your Discord account
      number, which kind of email it was and when it went. We do not keep the words of the email or
      the address it went to. We use it so a one-time email is never sent twice, and to keep the
      number of emails we send in a day under a limit. The notes for sign-in links and move codes
      are deleted after 90 days. The notes for the Try It emails are deleted a year after they
      were sent. All your notes are deleted when you delete your account.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Who sends them.</strong> The emails go out through Resend, a company that sends email
      for us. It gets the address an email goes to and the words in it, nothing else. Every Try It email ends with a link to stop emails like it and the sender's mailing address.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Improving DMbot</h2>
  <p>
    DMbot uses transcripts and what people type in the table's chat to get better. You can turn
    this off in <a href="/account">My Account</a>. It covers every server. DMbot still runs your
    games as usual. It just won't use your words to get better. Turning it off does not stop
    recording, which is separate: to stop being recorded, press 🗂️ Menu in DMbot's private message
    to you, then "Stop recording me".
  </p>
  <p>
    Account details (Discord account numbers, emails and payment records) are never published.
  </p>

  <h2>Companies that help us</h2>
  <p>We share only what each one needs to do its job:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Discord</strong>: where DMbot runs, and how you sign in.</li>
    <li><strong>Deepgram</strong>: turns the sound of people who said yes into text.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Anthropic</strong>: the AI that helps with names, rules and story notes. It gets the
      text it needs, not sound. That text isn't used to train Anthropic's AI.{
        SUPPORT_AI_LIVE ? anthropicAddition : ""
      }
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>{geminiPrivacy.name}</strong>: {geminiPrivacy.lead} {geminiPrivacy.body}
      {geminiPrivacy.country} <a href={geminiPrivacy.termsUrl} rel="noopener noreferrer">{geminiPrivacy.termsText}</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>ElevenLabs</strong>: turns the words of a recap into a spoken voice, and makes its
      music, only when you choose a voice or music for a recap. It gets only the recap's text and
      short descriptions of the music, never anyone's voice.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Voyage AI (part of MongoDB, Inc.)</strong>: helps DMbot find the story notes that are
      most like what you ask about, for example to remind the DM of a character from an earlier
      session. It gets the campaign's own text: story notes, names, DM notes and the lines of a
      session's transcript. It gets text only, never sound. Voyage AI does not use your campaign's
      text to train its models. DMbot keeps what Voyage AI sends back (a list of numbers that
      stands for the meaning of the text) only while it keeps that text, and deletes it when the
      text, or the campaign, is deleted.
    </li>
    <li><strong>{paymentProvider}</strong>: takes payments.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Resend</strong>: sends DMbot's emails. It gets the address an email goes to and the
      words in it, nothing else.
    </li>
    <li><strong>GitHub</strong>: where messages from the Say hello page are posted, for anyone to read.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cloudflare</strong>: delivers this website, and checks that a person (not a
      robot) sent a Say hello message; for that it sees your internet address.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>OVH US LLC (OVHcloud, U.S.A.)</strong>, a subsidiary of OVH Groupe: the server
      DMbot and its database run on. Its servers are in the United States.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Deepgram, Anthropic, ElevenLabs, Voyage AI (MongoDB), OVH US, Cloudflare, GitHub, Resend and {paymentProvider} are companies in the
    United States, so if you are in the EU or the UK, your information goes there when you use
    DMbot. {paymentProvider} is the seller of record and handles withdrawal rights, invoices and
    tax.
  </p>
  <p>We don't sell your information and we don't use it for ads.</p>
  <p>
    If a server uses its own AI or speech key, we keep that key locked (encrypted), use it only
    for that server, and never show it in full.
  </p>

  <QabotPrivacy live={QABOT_LIVE} />
  <RecapsPrivacy live={RECAPS_LIVE} audio={RECAP_AUDIO_LIVE} share={RECAP_SHARE_LIVE} />

  <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
  <p>A campaign is deleted this long after its last game:</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
  <table>
    <caption>How long a campaign is kept after its last game, by plan</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Plan</th><th scope="col">Kept after the last game</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {
        retentionRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{row.plan}</td>
            <td>{row.keep}</td>
          </tr>
        ))
      }
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    If a plan stops being paid, its campaigns are kept for {keepAfterStopPaying}. We message the
    game master on Discord {deletionWarnings} before anything is deleted.
    Backups people already downloaded are theirs to keep or delete.
  </p>
  <p>
    When you delete your account, we delete your account details. We keep only:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>your Discord account number, if you used the free Try It, so it can't be used twice;</li>
    <li>
      the reference numbers of your payments, with your Discord account number, so no payment is
      counted twice;
    </li>
    <li>
      if you were ever given free access: your Discord account number, each time it was given,
      changed or taken back, and when, so we can see who had free access.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    {paymentProvider}, as the seller, keeps the invoices and tax records that payment law
    requires.
  </p>

  <h2>Your choices</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Stop being recorded</strong> at any time: press 🗂️ Menu in DMbot's
      private message to you, then "Stop recording me", then "Yes". Or type
      <code>/consent revoke</code> in Discord. DMbot stops recording you and removes what this
      page says it removes. What DMbot already made from sessions you took part in, such as a
      recap or the story facts your game master kept, stays with the campaign. Your voice was
      not kept.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Delete your account</strong> from <a href="/account">My Account</a>. This removes
      your sign-in details and your plan, straight away. If you pay for a
      plan, you won't be charged again, and the rest of the month isn't paid back. If you want
      money back, ask before you delete (see <a href="/legal/refunds#stopping">Refunds</a>). Lines
      you said in
      someone else's campaign stay there, readable by that server, until that campaign is
      deleted. What DMbot made from those sessions stays with the campaign too: deleting your
      account does not remove it. Your campaigns are not deleted with your account, because the other players may
      still want to read or download them. They are deleted 120 days later, unless another DM
      of the campaign takes them over. If the owner's Discord account is gone, or the owner has
      been out of the server for 7 days, DMbot offers the campaign's other game masters a button
      to take the campaign on. Only a game master who presses it takes over.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Delete a campaign.</strong> Only the game master whose plan the campaign uses can
      (for a free campaign, the one who started it). Deleting it is the
      only thing that removes what DMbot made for it, such as its recaps, story facts and notes.
      A player who wants something removed can ask their game master.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Get a copy of your information.</strong>
      Press <strong>Download my data</strong> in <a href="/account">My Account</a>. You get one
      file with your account, plan and payments, where you said yes to recording, campaign names
      and character sheets.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Ask about your information.</strong> You can ask us to show you, correct, send you
      a copy of, or delete the information we hold about you, by emailing {operator.email}. We
      answer within 30 days.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Cookies</h2>
  <p>
    This website uses one cookie to keep you signed in, and two short ones (they last 10
    minutes) that are used only while you sign in or add DMbot to a server. Other websites
    can't read them. There are no tracking or ad cookies. The robot check on the Say hello
    page is run by Cloudflare and may use a cookie of its own to do its job.
  </p>

  <h2>Keeping it safe</h2>
  <p>
    Everything travels over a locked (encrypted) connection. Keys and secrets are stored
    encrypted, and our logs hold ids, never what you said or your payment details.
  </p>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>
    You must be at least {minimumAge} to use DMbot.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If we change what we keep or who sees it, we'll update this page and tell signed-in users. If
    the question you said yes to changes, DMbot asks you again before it records you.
  </p>
</LegalPage>
